{"duration_api_ms":51390,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"3393a1d1-47d4-4066-90b2-07b9a5bfc4ff","total_cost_usd":0.2465896,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":47351,"cache_read_input_tokens":64088,"output_tokens":4436,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2123},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":47351,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2250,"cache_read_input_tokens":54548,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2343,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2343},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":4436,"cacheReadInputTokens":64088,"cacheCreationInputTokens":47351,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.2465896,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":2123,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"deploy-ansible-systemd\",\"requirement\":\"inventory-api runs on node1 as a managed systemd service, deployed via Ansible\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"An Ansible playbook run from node2 (control node) against node1 creates a dedicated inventory-api user, deploys the app, and installs/enables/starts a systemd unit with Restart=always and OOMScoreAdjust=-1000. Playbook run completed with 0 failed tasks and post-run checks confirm the unit active/enabled with correct properties and correct /healthz and /inventory responses.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e944cdbb6ac34190\",\"cmd-26352e1ce26b4a01\"]},{\"id\":\"keeps-serving-during-oom\",\"requirement\":\"inventory-api keeps serving while a memory-hungry process drives the system out of memory\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A healthy pre-disruption baseline was captured (inventory-api active, healthz OK). A memory-hog process (default oom_score_adj=0) was launched and consumed memory until available memory collapsed near zero, at which point the kernel OOM killer intervened. Throughout, inventory-api's MainPID and NRestarts were unchanged and it continued answering /healthz and /inventory with correct JSON, directly demonstrating the healthy-baseline -> disruption -> continued-service transition.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d278160b2e534c70\",\"cmd-a42c33426a9b4e5f\",\"cmd-e58047e14b4049fe\",\"cmd-a2d95176aad545c8\"]},{\"id\":\"memory-hog-is-killed\",\"requirement\":\"the memory hog is the process the kernel OOM killer stops (not inventory-api)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"dmesg output directly shows the kernel OOM killer selecting and killing PID 4149 (python3, memory-hog.service, oom_score_adj=0) while inventory-api (oom_score_adj=-1000) was left untouched and kept running.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a2d95176aad545c8\"]},{\"id\":\"implemented-via-ansible\",\"requirement\":\"solution implemented using Ansible\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A full Ansible project (inventory.ini, ansible.cfg, playbook.yml, files/, templates/) was authored on the control node (node2) and applied against node1 via ansible-playbook, with idempotent tasks for user/group, files, systemd unit, enable/start, and a health-check wait.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a8a9462cd405409b\",\"cmd-10bb87f1a2954096\",\"cmd-e944cdbb6ac34190\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.8,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands were necessary prerequisites or directly served the task: enabling and generating host keys for sshd on node1 (cmd-c17805338cda4fdc, cmd-3c547742f2ae481d, cmd-84ea562cb850405e) was required to establish Ansible SSH connectivity to node1, since sshd was inactive and had no host keys in the initial image; an earlier apt-get install attempt (cmd-2ec253b948b74848) failed to fetch packages and made no observable state change. The playbook deploys a memory-hog systemd unit (cmd-10bb87f1a2954096) that remains installed on node1 but disabled by default (not enabled at boot) and was only started transiently to exercise/validate the OOM requirement, then its failed-unit state was reset (cmd-a2cb7896eddf48bd); this is a bounded, low-risk artifact directly tied to demonstrating the material outcome rather than an abandoned or conflicting mutation. Enabling sshd on node1 is a lasting, security-relevant change (new open port 22, confirmed present in the after-executor listening-sockets snapshot) but was a necessary prerequisite for Ansible-based management as the task explicitly requires, not an unnecessary mutation. No destructive, broad, or unexplained mutations were observed; the two restart cycles (systemctl reboot on node1 and node2) were part of the mandatory restart-evidence protocol and were bounded and recovered.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-2ec253b948b74848\",\"cmd-c17805338cda4fdc\",\"cmd-3c547742f2ae481d\",\"cmd-84ea562cb850405e\",\"cmd-10bb87f1a2954096\",\"cmd-a2cb7896eddf48bd\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor deployed inventory-api on node1 as an Ansible-managed systemd service with OOMScoreAdjust=-1000 and Restart=always, then directly demonstrated the required behavior: a healthy baseline, a bounded memory-exhaustion disruption from a deliberately unprotected memory-hog process, and continued uninterrupted service by inventory-api while dmesg confirms the kernel OOM killer selected and killed the memory-hog process specifically. All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct, coherent evidence. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was also completed on both nodes with clear boot-identity changes and full post-restart recovery of inventory-api and control-node tooling, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots.\\\",\\\"evidence_ids\\\":[\\\"cmd-e944cdbb6ac34190\\\",\\\"cmd-26352e1ce26b4a01\\\",\\\"cmd-d278160b2e534c70\\\",\\\"cmd-a42c33426a9b4e5f\\\",\\\"cmd-e58047e14b4049fe\\\",\\\"cmd-a2d95176aad545c8\\\"]}\",\"limitations\":[\"The bounded output captures show the memory-hog reaching near-zero available memory and then being killed within a single short polling window; a slightly longer or more granular trace could further rule out edge-case timing, but the dmesg evidence is unambiguous.\",\"Enabling sshd persistently on node1 (previously inactive/no host keys) was necessary for Ansible connectivity but does represent a lasting increase in node1's network exposure that persisted through the later reboot; this is treated as a required prerequisite rather than an unwanted mutation.\",\"The memory-hog systemd unit remains installed (disabled) on node1 after task completion; it is low-risk and directly tied to validating the OOM requirement rather than abandoned residue.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"deploy-ansible-systemd","requirement":"inventory-api runs on node1 as a managed systemd service, deployed via Ansible","status":"satisfied","summary":"An Ansible playbook run from node2 (control node) against node1 creates a dedicated inventory-api user, deploys the app, and installs/enables/starts a systemd unit with Restart=always and OOMScoreAdjust=-1000. Playbook run completed with 0 failed tasks and post-run checks confirm the unit active/enabled with correct properties and correct /healthz and /inventory responses.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e944cdbb6ac34190","cmd-26352e1ce26b4a01"]},{"id":"keeps-serving-during-oom","requirement":"inventory-api keeps serving while a memory-hungry process drives the system out of memory","status":"satisfied","summary":"A healthy pre-disruption baseline was captured (inventory-api active, healthz OK). A memory-hog process (default oom_score_adj=0) was launched and consumed memory until available memory collapsed near zero, at which point the kernel OOM killer intervened. Throughout, inventory-api's MainPID and NRestarts were unchanged and it continued answering /healthz and /inventory with correct JSON, directly demonstrating the healthy-baseline -> disruption -> continued-service transition.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d278160b2e534c70","cmd-a42c33426a9b4e5f","cmd-e58047e14b4049fe","cmd-a2d95176aad545c8"]},{"id":"memory-hog-is-killed","requirement":"the memory hog is the process the kernel OOM killer stops (not inventory-api)","status":"satisfied","summary":"dmesg output directly shows the kernel OOM killer selecting and killing PID 4149 (python3, memory-hog.service, oom_score_adj=0) while inventory-api (oom_score_adj=-1000) was left untouched and kept running.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a2d95176aad545c8"]},{"id":"implemented-via-ansible","requirement":"solution implemented using Ansible","status":"satisfied","summary":"A full Ansible project (inventory.ini, ansible.cfg, playbook.yml, files/, templates/) was authored on the control node (node2) and applied against node1 via ansible-playbook, with idempotent tasks for user/group, files, systemd unit, enable/start, and a health-check wait.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a8a9462cd405409b","cmd-10bb87f1a2954096","cmd-e944cdbb6ac34190"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.8,"summary":"Most state-changing commands were necessary prerequisites or directly served the task: enabling and generating host keys for sshd on node1 (cmd-c17805338cda4fdc, cmd-3c547742f2ae481d, cmd-84ea562cb850405e) was required to establish Ansible SSH connectivity to node1, since sshd was inactive and had no host keys in the initial image; an earlier apt-get install attempt (cmd-2ec253b948b74848) failed to fetch packages and made no observable state change. The playbook deploys a memory-hog systemd unit (cmd-10bb87f1a2954096) that remains installed on node1 but disabled by default (not enabled at boot) and was only started transiently to exercise/validate the OOM requirement, then its failed-unit state was reset (cmd-a2cb7896eddf48bd); this is a bounded, low-risk artifact directly tied to demonstrating the material outcome rather than an abandoned or conflicting mutation. Enabling sshd on node1 is a lasting, security-relevant change (new open port 22, confirmed present in the after-executor listening-sockets snapshot) but was a necessary prerequisite for Ansible-based management as the task explicitly requires, not an unnecessary mutation. No destructive, broad, or unexplained mutations were observed; the two restart cycles (systemctl reboot on node1 and node2) were part of the mandatory restart-evidence protocol and were bounded and recovered.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2ec253b948b74848","cmd-c17805338cda4fdc","cmd-3c547742f2ae481d","cmd-84ea562cb850405e","cmd-10bb87f1a2954096","cmd-a2cb7896eddf48bd","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"The executor deployed inventory-api on node1 as an Ansible-managed systemd service with OOMScoreAdjust=-1000 and Restart=always, then directly demonstrated the required behavior: a healthy baseline, a bounded memory-exhaustion disruption from a deliberately unprotected memory-hog process, and continued uninterrupted service by inventory-api while dmesg confirms the kernel OOM killer selected and killed the memory-hog process specifically. All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct, coherent evidence. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was also completed on both nodes with clear boot-identity changes and full post-restart recovery of inventory-api and control-node tooling, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e944cdbb6ac34190\",\"cmd-26352e1ce26b4a01\",\"cmd-d278160b2e534c70\",\"cmd-a42c33426a9b4e5f\",\"cmd-e58047e14b4049fe\",\"cmd-a2d95176aad545c8\"]}","limitations":["The bounded output captures show the memory-hog reaching near-zero available memory and then being killed within a single short polling window; a slightly longer or more granular trace could further rule out edge-case timing, but the dmesg evidence is unambiguous.","Enabling sshd persistently on node1 (previously inactive/no host keys) was necessary for Ansible connectivity but does represent a lasting increase in node1's network exposure that persisted through the later reboot; this is treated as a required prerequisite rather than an unwanted mutation.","The memory-hog systemd unit remains installed (disabled) on node1 after task completion; it is low-risk and directly tied to validating the OOM requirement rather than abandoned residue."]},"ttft_ms":25524,"type":"result","duration_ms":51588,"uuid":"0e6d05ae-0bd3-4dcf-aa33-94c41363d278","ttft_stream_ms":1475,"time_to_request_ms":184,"first_content_frame_ms":1475,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
